hi there uh  i want to talk today about freedom of  expression  and particularly uh as it applies to a  recent controversy we've had here in in  canada  where uh leading magazine published an  article which was originally entitled  too asian  and it talked about the fact that some  um  white if you want non-asian students  didn't want to go to  certain universities where there was a  high concentration of  students of asian origin whether they  are  first generation parents or been in  canada for generations  and the white students are quoted in the  article as saying that they felt the  asian students studied too hard and that  the white students again quoted in the  article preferred to party or not study  quite so hard or didn't want to have to  compete with these hard-working asian  students and some asian students are  quoted as saying that they prefer  to be with  other asian students because the white  students spend so much time drinking at  parties and so forth  it wasn't a very brilliant or deep  article  it did describe a fairly widespread  phenomenon and that is that first of all  students of asian origin particularly  east asian and particularly chinese  because in canada that's the largest  source of or ethnic origin if you want  of people of asian origin  um  that they are  relative to their share of the  population they're over represented in  our universities  and this reflects the fact that they  study harder and of course therefore  they have every right to be in the  universities because the universities  uh at the present time  acceptance or admittance is based on  your marks which is probably the right  thing to do  but there are people who don't like it  who feel that  you know it was more fun when  universities were more about partying  probably though uh not all white kids  react the way the kids in this article  uh seem to feel  uh i am sure there are asian  origin kids who prefer to go to a  university where there's more partying  so there's a certain amount of  exaggeration or or stereotyping but it's  a story i mean  every story i read in a magazine it  doesn't strike me necessarily as being  accurate  or brilliant and this one probably  is not brilliant but it does reflect a  widespread attitude  now what's come up now as a result of  this is there are all kinds of  chinese canadians  who are insisting that this magazine  should be boycotted we have a professor  at the university here in vancouver  henry yu who's  more or less encouraging his students to  boycott  uh  the owner of the magazine which is a  company that's involved in  in the distribution of  mobile telephones  uh we have a senator  who stood up and said you know  because in canada again it's not a  policy that i agree with but the  government subsidizes canadian  publications in order that they might  survive against american competition  mclean's magazine which is the magazine  involved in this  article  gets a million and a half dollars from  the government  so the senator vivian poi is saying that  money should be withdrawn because of  this terrible article  what bothers me in this is this  tremendous group pressure  from various vocal  people in this chinese  canadian largely the chinese community  who are trying to shut down  what is essentially the freedom of  expression of the magazine  uh there's nothing in that article  that is uh  you know anti-anybody  um  you know i henry you for example this  professor at ubc who is of chinese  origin i mean he wrote an article uh in  the local newspaper here in vancouver  where he said that vancouver is becoming  an asian city  and he lamented the fact that a certain  part of vancouver  doesn't get it and is still quote  according to him as white as the driven  snow so like he's bothered by the fact  that some areas of vancouver are still  not  asian enough for him to me it's just a  silly comment but  i'm not suggesting that the vancouver  sun should be boycotted because they  printed an article written by henry yu  you know there are asians who say  chinese who choose to live where there  are lots of chinese there are others who  specifically choose to live where there  aren't a lot of chinese for whatever  reason the first group because they feel  more comfortable with other asians and  the second group because they want to  give they want to give their kids  perhaps a a different experience and not  to to be living only with with their own  kind it is an unfortunate fact that in  our schools and universities we do do  tend to see people kind of grouping  together in cliques based on their  ethnic origin i think that's too bad but  it is a fact  and to report on that  is not grounds for a massive  uh sort of uh  indignation by any particular ethnic  group and i think it's scary  that uh  every time a group feels that they're in  some way offended by an article  uh that they have the right  to boycott it demand that its funding be  taken away  uh boycott uh you know the owners who  are in other businesses in other words  to try and  uh  gang up on and pressure  uh these publications to produce  articles that they like  i mean they have every right to write  uh letters to that magazine to comment  in other publications to do whatever  they want be as indignant and offended  as they want to be no one can prevent  them from doing that but it's the idea  that they're gonna  through their collective muscle  force  these publications to to dance to their  tune that's pretty scary stuff  and they've been able to get some of the  more quote progressive  uh municipalities in canada like in  victoria and vancouver and they tried it  in toronto to get the municipal  government to come out and make a  statement condemning this  uh  magazine article i mean has that  municipal government in victoria got  nothing better to do than to worry about  what is written  in a magazine  uh on on things that have nothing to do  with their municipality  besides which if you read the article  there is nothing particularly  offensive  nothing censorable in the article to  most objective observers  so  uh you know i in so far as canada is  concerned  i mean that that asians have a  reputation of being hard-working and  studious and not  party animals  uh there's probably some truth to that  that's not to say that all asians are  that way  yeah it's unfortunate that the term  asian is used indiscriminately to apply  both to people who just arrived here and  people who have been here for five  generations but  the vast majority of asians in our  people of asian origin in fact did  arrive the last 30 years in so far as  canada is concerned i think that's a  good thing personally uh i think that  canada  i don't believe in multiculturalism per  se i most of the people of asian origin  that i see young people they speak  english and and overwhelmingly their  culture is not that different from that  of of other canadians  and uh i think it's a good thing that  the people gradually get used to the  idea that  someone  who's canadian  can have a a funny name you know like  when i was a kid growing up my name is  kaufman  that wasn't quite as good as  smith jones or even la pierre because  originally canada was like english and  french so if you had a polish or german  or italian name that was kind of second  class that's no longer the case well by  the same token you may look uh indian  you may look chinese you may look  african you're just as canadian and i  think people are gradually getting used  to that but still  ah  there it remains true that people of in  certain groups  do tend to stick together which i think  is a little unfortunate  and uh and as a result that can cause  certain reactions amongst other people  in in in the community and so  newspapers and magazines should be  allowed to write about that  in this particular magazine  uh the mclean's magazine the article  didn't say  that we should reduce or limit or  restrict the number of asians at  university there's no such implication  at all if if anything it suggested that  uh that this was not the correct  approach and and i think it almost  implied that the whites were lazy or or  jealous or just didn't work hard enough  or whatever anyway there is nothing  there but the and uh i should add  that some of the loudest spokespeople  here like henry you i mean he strikes me  as a raving uh ethnic chauvinist  for for sort of  chinese canadianism i mean he published  he recently received i should add now  that i can go 15 minutes on itunes  on youtube  he managed to receive 900 000  from the federal government for a  chinese canadian history portal 900 000  and in accepting the money he said yes  you know we have to reassess how we look  at the history of canada because after  all he said a hundred years before  confederation there were two chinese  sailors on a ship that arrived on the  west coast of uh  canada  and so really uh the chinese were  interacting with the aboriginals here  at the same time as the europeans and so  the chinese are also a founding people  well that's just pure  uh nonsensical historical revisionism in  fact the europeans  were settled here  in the 1600s i mean john cabot was an  italian he was here in the 1500s he was  the captain of a ship we have basks  settling around the uh  the east coast basque fishermen we had  the massive arrivals of united empire  loyalists including germans and dutch  all before these two chinese sailors  showed up on a boat  but insofar as founding people every  country has its its myth of origin and  it's it's historical sort of founding  uh traditions and canada speaks english  and french and in the early years it was  this interaction between the the british  the french and the natives that formed  the core of what canada became through  its institutions and languages and so  forth that's not to say that we haven't  had all kinds of  italian chinese ukrainian german  japanese  jamaican  you name it indian others come here and  contribute of course they do but they  all end up speaking english and they all  conform to this  uh canadian  culture  that was originally uh established by  those founding people so  i  come from i was not born here i was born  in in sweden you know 60 years 62 years  ago  i came over here when i was five but my  family  conformed to the culture that was  already here so we are not a founding  people  uh similarly this vivian poi lady who is  a senator uh i mean i found a speech of  her she gave for the progressive muslims  society of canada  which is just full of the most amazing  stuff like she said tells the  progressive muslims that  uh you know she's a canadian senator  lived here for 50 years she says uh yeah  i'm  in my  uncle's company in hong kong we had a  muslim employee  and all i know is that muslims they're  just like us they look like chinese  except they don't eat pork well that's  not true because a lot of muslims in  china don't look chinese so she's kind  of wiped them out you know and then she  was on about how powerful the ming navy  was had ships that were larger than  columbus's ships and she fantasizes that  um you know if the ming navy had come  across the portuguese ships they would  have crushed them well no they didn't  they didn't the portuguese shift arrived  in ming china and founded macau and they  did their ships weren't crushed but yes  the chinese ships were larger so then  there's more of this kind of stuff  anyway  i think we're  i just  shudder at this sort of ethnic  chauvinism that's springing up and it's  its ability to  garner government funding  and to put pressure on  on uh  publications to try to force them to  apologize or to shut off their funding  scary stuff i hope it dies soon anyway  there's my  christmas boxing day rant before i head  off skiing thanks for listening bye for  now

